  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1241 PM MDT Sun Sep 6 2026\n\nA few showers and thunderstorms continue early this afternoon\nnear the Salmon River, but will increase from the south later\ntoday. Through tonight, we will continue to see coverage\nincreasing from the south, and separately across the central\nmountains with the low itself. There is a MARGINAL RISK for\nstorms through tonight potentially producing 60+ mph winds. The\nbest chance for gusts over 35 mph are across the Snake Plain,\nMagic Valley, and southeast highlands. The potential for severe\nstorms in mainly confined to the southeast highlands, with a few\nstorms elsewhere before midnight producing strong winds. With a\nlack of moisture really from Marie, rainfall amounts continue to\ndwindle each forecast. The consensus is that most areas will see\nanywhere from a couple of hundredths to about 0.15\". Across the\nsoutheast corner, the Big Holes, and north of Stanley through \nthe Frank Church, up 0.30\" is possible. There is a 40-60% chance\nof exceeding the end of the range in those areas. This is\nplausible especially if we get strong/severe storms going and/or\nmultiple storms moving over the same area. Any risk for\nlocalized flooding/debris is VERY LOW at the moment given the \nwaning rainfall rates and totals. Showers and storms will end\nlate tomorrow afternoon, although a couple of higher resolution\nmodels want to pop off a couple of thunderstorms north of\nChallis and along the Montana border. Outside of thunderstorms,\nwe will see fairly gusty south winds today, switch to the west\nand southwest tonight and Labor Day. Gusts in the 25-35 mph\nrange are possible at times, with gusts above 40 mph across the\nArco Desert behind the cold front. Temperatures will be 10-20 \ndegrees COLDER tomorrow, 50s and 60s for the valleys. We may see\na few low 70s south of the Snake Plain toward the Utah border.\nWith the low crossing the central mountains and the coldest\nair associated with that, we are still going to see a period of\nrain and snow above 10,000ft tonight and tomorrow morning!  \n\nWe will quickly dry out for midweek. The Blend of Models wants\nto keep us dry, or at least any chance of showers or storms\nbelow 10% now through Friday. We will be sandwiched between low\npressure to our north and a building high to our south. There is\nsome tropical moisture that works around the ridge and comes in\nfor Thursday and Friday, and believe it not COULD tap into the\nremants of Marie for a slight boost. The latter remains to be\nseem since it will be absorbed into a bigger low offshore, but\nthere does seem to be some connection to other sources of\ntropical moisture as well. All of that means that don't be\nsurprise to see the Blend of Models eventually increase the\nchance of showers and storms if these trends continue to show\nup. Even if we get some moisture in here, highs quickly push\nback into the 70s and 80s by Wednesday. The better chance of\nshowers and storms should hold off until next weekend as low\npressure moves inland and crosses the northern Rockies.",
